Chapter: IdtInspectTest
  cites Dev chapter IdtInspector
  cites Dev chapter HexFormat

 The interrupt inspector against the table the boot path actually built.

 NO ABSOLUTE HANDLER ADDRESS IS ASSERTED, deliberately. A gate's offset is
 the address of a stub in this binary, so an expected file naming one
 would have to be rewritten every time anything above it in the image
 moved, and it would be measuring the linker rather than the inspector.
 What is stable is the RELATIONSHIP the emitter guarantees: the stubs are
 twelve bytes apart, so vector one is twelve past vector zero and vector
 thirty-two is three hundred and eighty-four past it. That pins the
 assembly of the offset out of its three separate fields, which is the
 part of the decode that can actually be wrong, and it does it without
 naming a number that churns.

 THE ABSENT-GATE CASE IS MANUFACTURED, because the machine will not
 supply one. All 256 gates are present on a booted system, so
 `NOT PRESENT` and the unknown-type fallback are arms no reading of a
 healthy table can reach; an inspector that had them hardwired the wrong
 way would pass a test that only ever looked at real entries. Vector 255
 is written to zero, read back, and restored, and the restored read is
 printed too -- without it a poke that failed and a decode that ignored
 the attribute byte would look the same.

 THE MASK LINES ARE THE DETERMINISTIC HALF OF THE CONTROLLER READ. IRR
 and ISR move as interrupts arrive and retire, so asserting them would be
 asserting where in a timer period the test happened to run. The two
 masks are written once by `emit-pic-init` and never again, and they are
 also the more interesting reading: the master's `#ec` leaves the timer,
 the keyboard and COM1 unmasked, and the cascade line MASKED, which with
 the slave's `#ff` says the second controller cannot deliver anything at
 all. Whether that is intended is not this test's question; that it is
 the state, and that the inspector reports it, is.

 We say:

Section: Joining

  joined : List Text, Integer, Text -> Text
  joined (xs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length xs then acc
    else if text-length acc == 0 then joined xs (i + 1) (list-at xs i)
    else joined xs (i + 1) (acc & " | " & list-at xs i)

Section: Counting Present Gates

  present-count : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  present-count (vec) (acc) =
    if vec >= 256 then acc
    else if ii-gate-present (ii-entry-addr vec) then present-count (vec + 1) (acc + 1)
    else present-count (vec + 1) acc

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing
  opening = act
    let a0 = ii-entry-addr 0
    in let a1 = ii-entry-addr 1
    in let a8 = ii-entry-addr 8
    in let a32 = ii-entry-addr 32
    in let a255 = ii-entry-addr 255
    in let saved = peek-byte a255 5
    in act
      print-line-uni ("sel0: #" & u16-to-hex (ii-gate-selector a0))
      print-line-uni ("attr0: " & ii-gate-attrs a0)
      print-line-uni ("attr8: " & ii-gate-attrs a8)
      print-line-uni ("attr32: " & ii-gate-attrs a32)
      print-line-uni ("stride1: " & show (ii-gate-offset a1 - ii-gate-offset a0))
      print-line-uni ("stride32: " & show (ii-gate-offset a32 - ii-gate-offset a0))
      print-line-uni ("present-count: " & show (present-count 0 0))

      let w = poke-byte a255 5 0
      in print-line-uni ("absent255: " & ii-gate-attrs a255)

      let r = poke-byte a255 5 saved
      in print-line-uni ("restored255: " & ii-gate-attrs a255)

      print-line-uni ("low: " & joined (ii-entry-report (0 - 1)) 0 "")
      print-line-uni ("high: " & joined (ii-entry-report 256) 0 "")

      print-line-uni ("key: " & ii-key-line)
      print-line-uni ("fault: " & joined ii-fault-report 0 "")

      irq <- ii-irq-report
      print-line-uni ("master-imr: " & list-at irq 2)
      print-line-uni ("slave-imr: " & list-at irq 5)
    end
  end
